WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:10.680
Here is the cast. The pentoses ribose and deoxyribose build your nucleic acids; the

2
00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.180
only difference is one missing hydroxyl.

3
00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:20.820
The hexoses glucose, mannose, and galactose differ at just one carbon each mannose

4
00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:25.800
at C2, galactose at C4 which makes them epimers of glucose.

5
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:32.560
And fructose, off to the side, is the odd one: a ketose, with its carbonyl at carbon two.

6
00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:36.620
Six sugars carry most of the biological traffic.

7
00:00:37.483 --> 00:00:39.783
Why don't sugars stay straight?

8
00:00:40.663 --> 00:00:46.403
The chemistry is simple: an aldehyde reacts with an alcohol to form a hemiacetal,

9
00:00:46.823 --> 00:00:50.243
and a ketone with an alcohol to form a hemiketal.

10
00:00:51.103 --> 00:00:57.943
In an aldohexose like glucose, the sugar's own hydroxyl on carbon five swings around

11
00:00:57.943 --> 00:01:04.223
and attacks its carbonyl at carbon one, closing the molecule into a ring no enzyme

12
00:01:04.223 --> 00:01:06.383
needed, like a self-closing gate.

13
00:01:06.383 --> 00:01:10.723
That ring-closure is what creates the anomeric carbon.

14
00:01:11.567 --> 00:01:13.547
Watch glucose close.

15
00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:21.187
The open-chain aldehyde folds so the carbon-five hydroxyl reaches the carbonyl at

16
00:01:21.187 --> 00:01:24.007
carbon one, and the ring snaps shut.

17
00:01:24.747 --> 00:01:31.127
The old carbonyl carbon becomes a brand-new chiral center, the anomeric carbon, and

18
00:01:31.127 --> 00:01:37.487
it can point two ways: the alpha form has the C1 hydroxyl below the ring, the beta

19
00:01:37.487 --> 00:01:43.867
form above. In solution glucose interconverts between them through the open chain,

20
00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:47.427
settling near one-third alpha and two-thirds beta.

21
00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:51.200
This is the whole chapter on one slide.

22
00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:54.060
Take identical glucose monomers.

23
00:01:54.740 --> 00:02:00.400
Link them with α-1,4 bonds and the chain coils into a compact, digestible

24
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.400
storage polymer: starch in plants, glycogen in animals.

25
00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:11.140
Link the very same monomers with β-1,4 bonds and the chains lie flat, hydrogen-bond

26
00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:16.100
into rigid fibers, and become cellulose, indigestible structure.

27
00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.840
One bond geometry, opposite destinies.

28
00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:25.400
The wood in your pencil and the glucose in your blood are the same brick, laid a

29
00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:26.400
different way.

30
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:29.380
Glycogen's branching is the point.

31
00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:36.620
α-1,4 chains run the length, and an α-1,6 branch sprouts about every ten

32
00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:43.820
glucose units. Stack that up and a single liver glycogen molecule can reach 55,000

33
00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:47.960
glucose units with about 2,100 non-reducing ends.

34
00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:49.780
Why does that matter?

35
00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:57.240
Enzymes only pull glucose from the ends, so 2,100 ends means many enzymes can work

36
00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:03.220
at once. Branching is parallel processing for energy: it's what lets you mobilize

37
00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:04.900
glucose fast when you sprint.
